{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And the sons of Jacob answered Shechem and Hamor his father with deception</b> — Now, Hamor and Shechem spoke to her father and to her brothers, but the old man did not answer them a word, for his sons would speak in his place in this matter, out of respect for him — since the matter was a disgrace to them, they did not want him to open his mouth to speak of it at all.\n\nThere is a question here, for the matter appears as though they answered with her father's consent and by his counsel, since they were in his presence, and he knew their answer — that they would speak with deception. And if so, why was he angry? Furthermore, it is inconceivable that it would be his will to give his daughter in marriage to a Canaanite who had defiled her. And behold, all the brothers gave this answer with deception, yet Simeon and Levi alone carried out the deed, and the father cursed their anger at them alone. \n\nThe answer is that the deception was in their saying to them that every male should be circumcised, for they thought that the men of the city would not do so, and if perhaps they would listen to their prince and all of them be circumcised, they would come on the third day when they were in pain and take their daughter from the house of Shechem. This was the counsel of all the brothers, and with their father's permission; but Simeon and Levi sought to take vengeance upon them, and they killed every man of the city.\n\nIt is possible that Jacob's anger was because he cursed their wrath over the fact that they killed the men of the city who had not sinned against him — and the fitting thing for them would have been to kill Shechem alone. This is what Scripture says: \"And the sons of Jacob answered Shechem and Hamor his father with deception, and spoke — because he had defiled Dinah their sister\" — for all of them agreed to speak to him with deception because of the outrage he had committed against them.\n\nNow many will ask: how could the sons of Jacob, the righteous ones, have done this deed, to shed innocent blood? The Rav answered in the Book of Judges (Maimonides, Laws of Kings 14:9) and said that the sons of Noah are commanded concerning laws (dinim), which is to appoint judges in every district to judge according to their six commandments, and a son of Noah who transgresses any one of them is killed by the sword; one who saw another transgress one of them and they did not judge him to be killed — behold, this witness is killed by the sword. And because of this, all the people of Shechem were liable to execution, since Shechem robbed and they saw and knew and did not judge him.\n\nThese words are not correct in my eyes, for if so, Jacob our father should have been first and foremost in justifying their death. And if he feared them, why was he angry with his sons and cursed their wrath after some time, and punished them and divided them and scattered them? After all, they were in the right and performed a commandment and trusted in Elohim, and He delivered them.\n\nIn my opinion, the laws commanded to the sons of Noah in their seven commandments are not merely to appoint judges in every district alone, but He commanded them concerning the laws of theft and fraud and oppression and the wages of a hired worker, and the laws of bailees and coercion and seduction and principal categories of damages and one who wounds another, and the laws of lender and borrower and the laws of purchase and sale and similar matters — like the nature of the laws with which Israel was commanded. And one is executed for them if he stole or oppressed or coerced or seduced the daughter of another, or set fire to his stack and wounded him, and similar things. Included in this commandment is that they must appoint judges in every city as well, just as Israel must. And if they did not do so, they are not executed — for this is a positive commandment incumbent upon them, and they said (Sanhedrin 57a) only that their warning is their execution, and a \"warning\" is not called except a prevention by a negative commandment. And thus is the way of the Gemara in Sanhedrin (59a) and in the Jerusalem Talmud: they said concerning the laws of Noah — he perverted his judgment: he is killed; he took a bribe: he is killed. In the laws of Israel, any case from which you know you are exempt, you are permitted to flee from, but in their laws, even though you know you are exempt, you are permitted to flee from it. It appears from here that a gentile is permitted to say to the litigants: I am not available to you — for the addition \"you shall not be partial before any man\" (Deuteronomy 1:17) — do not hold back your words on account of any man (Sanhedrin 6b) — is only in Israel. All the more so that he is not killed when he does not make himself an officer, enforcer, and ruler to judge his master.\n\nAnd what would the Rav seek to obligate them for? Were the men of Shechem and all the seven nations not idolaters and practitioners of forbidden sexual relations and doers of all the abominations of HaShem? And Scripture cries out against them in several places (Deuteronomy 12:2): \"upon the high mountains and upon the hills and under every leafy tree,\" etc.; \"you shall not learn to do\" etc. (ibid. 18:9); and concerning forbidden relations (Leviticus 18:27): \"for all these abominations have they done,\" etc. — except that the matter is not given over to Jacob and his sons to execute judgment upon them. \n\nBut the matter of Shechem: the sons of Jacob, because the men of Shechem were wicked and their blood was considered in their eyes like water, sought to take vengeance upon them with a sword of vengeance, and they killed the king and every man of his city, for they are his servants and subject to his authority. And the covenant of which they were circumcised was not considered in their eyes as anything at all, for it was done to flatter their master.\n\nAnd Jacob said to them here that they had brought him into danger, as it says: \"you have troubled me, to make me odious\" (Genesis 34:30). And he cursed their wrath because they did violence to the men of the city, for they said to them in his presence: \"and we will dwell with you and become one people\" (Genesis 34:16), and they were choosing them and committed to their word, and perhaps they would return to HaShem — yet they killed them gratuitously, for they had done them no harm at all. This is what he said: \"weapons of violence are their trade\" (Genesis 49:5).\n\nAnd if we are to believe the Book of \"The Wars of the Sons of Jacob\" (i.e., Sefer haYashar), terror came upon their father when the neighbors of Shechem gathered and fought three great wars against them, and had not their father girded his weapons of war as well and fought against them, they would have been in danger, as recounted in that book.\n\nAnd our Rabbis (Genesis Rabbah 80:10) alluded to this in the verse: \"which I took from the hand of the Amorite with my sword and with my bow\" (Genesis 48:22) — they said: all the surrounding areas gathered to engage them in battle, and Jacob girded weapons of war against them, as Rashi wrote there. But Scripture abbreviates this matter, for it was a hidden miracle, since they were mighty men and it was as though their own arm had saved them — just as Scripture abbreviated the matter of Abraham in Ur Kasdim, and did not mention the war of Esau with the Horites at all. But it did mention here that there was a terror of Elohim upon the cities that were around them, so that they did not all gather to pursue the sons of Jacob, for they would have fallen upon them like the sand upon the seashore in abundance. And this is the meaning of \"terror of Elohim\" (Genesis 35:5) — for dread and fear fell upon them from the might that they saw in the battle.\n\nTherefore it says (Genesis 35:6): \"And Jacob came to Luz, that is, Bethel, he and all the people who were with him\" — to inform us that not one of them, nor any of their servants, was missing in the battle."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:20.842881512Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}